IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ddt-dates`

-- Dust Diseases Tribunal Act 1989 (NSW) -- the claims machinery, from the seat of the person who claims: the person
-- suffering (or who suffered) from a dust-related condition, or, after that person's death, their executor or
-- administrator or a relative claiming through them.
--
-- SEAT: the claimant (the plaintiff). Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Is my condition a
-- dust-related condition?", "Must my claim go to the Dust Diseases Tribunal?", "Is my claim out of time?", "Can my estate
-- still recover for my pain and suffering if I die?", "Does settling with one defendant stop me suing the others?", "Is
-- my damages award reduced by my dust diseases compensation?", "Can the Tribunal take my evidence early?", "Can I get
-- an interim payment while the insurers argue?", "Can I appeal?". The Tribunal, its registrar, the Supreme Court and the
-- insurers get no seat: their opinions, leave and discretions are inputs, answered conditionally.
--
-- Provisions: s 3 (dust-related condition, settlement), Sch 1; s 10(1), (6), (7) (exclusive jurisdiction; companies under
-- external administration); s 11(1)-(5) (claims to be brought in the Tribunal; who may claim); s 11A (provisional damages); s 12 (transfer from the Supreme and District
-- Courts); s 12A (no limitation period); s 12B (non-economic damages after the plaintiff's death); s 12C(1) (settlement
-- with one joint tortfeasor); s 12D (no deduction of dust diseases compensation); s 13(5) (no appeal but s 32); s 14
-- (money judgments); s 17(1), (4) (serving one of those jointly liable; personal representatives); s 19 (appearance and
-- fees); s 20(1), (6) (subpoenas and conduct money); s 22(2), (4) (copies of retained documents); s 24 (evidence taken
-- early); s 25(3), 25A, 25B (evidence, material and general issues from other proceedings); s 32 (appeal); s 32I(1), (2),
-- (4), (8) (claim information and its confidentiality); ss 41, 43, 44, 45 (interim payments). Out, with reasons in
-- NOTES.md: the Tribunal's constitution and staff (Part 2), the Rule Committee (Part 3A), regulation and rule-making
-- powers (ss 32H, 33, 34, 35), arbitration between insurers (Part 5), s 46 (reimbursement between insurers), the
-- defendants' and tortfeasors' provisions (ss 11(1A), 12C(2)), the Tribunal's own procedural powers (ss 13(1)-(4), (6),
-- (7), 17(5), 20(2)-(5), 21, 26), Sch 2 and the transitional Sch 3.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no question answers JUST TRUE, JUST FALSE where the Act's own words say no (a "means" definition, a
-- condition introduced by "if", "where", "unless", "except" or "on", a flat "may not", "is not", "must not"), or NOTHING
-- where the Act does not answer on the facts given; each NOTHING carries a "NOTHING-kind" comment.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; version as at 15 November 2024; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for
-- public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Dust Diseases Tribunal Act 1989`

§§ `The nouns`

-- Schedule 1 "Prescribed dust diseases", in the Schedule's order, and s 3 "dust-related condition" para (b) ("any other
-- pathological condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust").
DECLARE `The condition` IS ONE OF
    `aluminosis`
    `asbestosis`
    `asbestos induced carcinoma`
    `asbestos related pleural diseases`
    `bagassosis`
    `berylliosis`
    `byssinosis`
    `coal dust pneumoconiosis`
    `diffuse dust-related pulmonary fibrosis`
    `farmers lung`
    `hard metal pneumoconiosis`
    `hypersensitivity pneumonitis`
    `mesothelioma`
    `pneumoconiosis of any form`
    `silica-induced carcinoma of the lung`
    `silicosis`
    `silico-tuberculosis`
    `systemic sclerosis`
    `talcosis`
    `another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust`
    `a condition of some other part of the body attributable to dust`
    `a condition not attributable to dust`

-- s 11(1)(c), (5) and s 17(4): who is bringing the claim.
DECLARE `Who is claiming` IS ONE OF
    `the person with the condition`
    `their executor, administrator or other legal personal representative`
    `a relative for whose benefit a Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 action may be brought`
    `another person claiming through the person with the condition`
    `someone with no claim through the person with the condition`

-- s 12: the court the proceedings are in, and when they began.
DECLARE `The court my case is in` IS ONE OF
    `the Supreme Court`
    `the District Court`
    `some other court or tribunal`

DECLARE `When my case began` IS ONE OF
    `brought after section 12 commenced`
    `pending when section 12 commenced, its hearing not yet begun`
    `pending when section 12 commenced, its hearing already begun`

-- s 12B(1): the heads of damages the section names (those s 2(2)(d) of the 1944 Act otherwise takes from an estate).
DECLARE `The head of damages after death` IS ONE OF
    `the person's pain or suffering`
    `bodily or mental harm the person suffered`
    `curtailment of the person's expectation of life`
    `some other head of damages`

-- s 12D(3): the heads of "damages for non-economic loss", and the rest.
DECLARE `The part of my damages` IS ONE OF
    `pain and suffering`
    `loss of amenities of life`
    `loss of expectation of life`
    `disfigurement`
    `domestic, nursing or attendance services provided to me by another person`
    `economic loss or expenses`

DECLARE `The compensation paid or payable` IS ONE OF
    `compensation under the Workers' Compensation (Dust Diseases) Act 1942`
    `some other compensation or benefit`

-- s 14: what a Tribunal money judgment becomes.
DECLARE `What my money judgment becomes` IS ONE OF
    `taken to be a Supreme Court judgment for the money, and no filing fee is payable`
    `not yet: file the certified copy and the affidavit in the Common Law Division registry of the Supreme Court`

-- s 19: who appears for me.
DECLARE `Who appears for me` IS ONE OF
    `I appear myself`
    `an Australian legal practitioner appears for me`
    `someone who is not an Australian legal practitioner appears for me`

DECLARE `Who is paid for representing me` IS ONE OF
    `an Australian legal practitioner representing me`
    `someone else representing me`

-- s 25(3): the kind of evidence admitted in other proceedings.
DECLARE `The evidence from another case` IS ONE OF
    `historical evidence concerning dust exposure and dust diseases`
    `general medical evidence concerning dust exposure and dust diseases`
    `evidence particular to the other case`

-- s 32: the ground of appeal and the kind of decision.
DECLARE `The ground of my appeal` IS ONE OF
    `a point of law`
    `the admission or rejection of evidence`
    `a question of fact only`

DECLARE `The decision I would appeal` IS ONE OF
    `an interlocutory decision`
    `a decision as to costs only`
    `a final decision`
    `a decision made with the consent of the parties`

DECLARE `My appeal` IS ONE OF
    `I may appeal to the Supreme Court as of right`
    `I may appeal to the Supreme Court only with its leave`
    `no appeal: section 13(5) leaves only section 48 of the Supreme Court Act 1970`

-- s 32I: who must give the Registrar the claim information, and whether it has been given in time.
DECLARE `Who must give the claim information` IS ONE OF
    `my solicitor, not my barrister`
    `my Australian legal practitioner`
    `I must, because no Australian legal practitioner represents me`

DECLARE `What section 32I(4) means for me` IS ONE OF
    `section 32I(4) puts no duty on me, because a legal practitioner represents me`
    `I gave the information within the time`
    `I have not given the information within the time: an offence, maximum penalty 5 penalty units`
    `I have not yet given the information, and the time has not ended`

DECLARE `Why the information would be recorded or divulged` IS ONE OF
    `with the consent of the person who provided it`
    `in connection with the administration or execution of the Act`
    `a requirement of a NSW or Commonwealth law, other than a subpoena or other compulsory process`
    `a subpoena or other compulsory process`
    `some other reason`

-- s 10(6), (7): whose leave is needed against a company under external administration.
DECLARE `Whose leave I need against the company` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal's leave, not the leave of the Court supervising the administration`

-- Part 6: interim payments.
DECLARE `Interim payments for me` IS ONE OF
    `the Tribunal may order a designated insurer to make interim payments to me under section 43`
    `section 43 applies, but none of its conditions for an order is met`
    `Division 5 of Part 6 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 extends to my claim under section 41`

-- The parties and acts of the regulative rules.
DECLARE `Tribunal party` IS ONE OF `the claimant`
DECLARE `Tribunal act` IS ONE OF
    `bring proceedings before the Tribunal`
    `tender conduct money to the person subpoenaed` HAS `the sum tendered` IS A NUMBER
    `give the claim information to the Registrar`

§§ `s 3, Sch 1 -- dust-related condition`

-- s 3(1) "dust-related condition means--(a) a disease specified in Schedule 1, or (b) any other pathological condition of
-- the lungs, pleura or peritoneum that is attributable to dust." A closed "means" definition: anything outside both
-- paragraphs is JUST FALSE. Schedule 1 may be added to by order (s 35); the list is the deposited one.
@export Is my condition a "dust-related condition" under the Dust Diseases Tribunal Act (s 3, Schedule 1)?
GIVEN `my condition` IS A `The condition` @desc The disease or condition you have (or the person who died had). One of: "aluminosis", "asbestosis", "asbestos induced carcinoma", "asbestos related pleural diseases", "bagassosis", "berylliosis", "byssinosis", "coal dust pneumoconiosis", "diffuse dust-related pulmonary fibrosis", "farmers lung", "hard metal pneumoconiosis", "hypersensitivity pneumonitis", "mesothelioma", "pneumoconiosis of any form", "silica-induced carcinoma of the lung", "silicosis", "silico-tuberculosis", "systemic sclerosis", "talcosis", "another condition of the lungs, pleura or peritoneum attributable to dust", "a condition of some other part of the body attributable to dust", "a condition not attributable to dust"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my condition a dust-related condition` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my condition`
    WHEN `a condition of some other part of the body attributable to dust` THEN JUST FALSE  -- a "means" definition: para (b) is limited to the lungs, pleura and peritoneum
    WHEN `a condition not attributable to dust` THEN JUST FALSE  -- a "means" definition
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

GIVEN `my condition` IS A `The condition`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a dust-related condition` `my condition` IF
    `Is my condition a dust-related condition` `my condition` EQUALS JUST TRUE

§§ `ss 10(1), 11 -- claims that must be brought in the Tribunal`

-- s 11(1) "If--(a) a person is suffering, or has suffered, from a dust-related condition or a person who has died was,
-- immediately before death, suffering from a dust-related condition, and (b) it is alleged that the dust-related
-- condition was attributable or partly attributable to a breach of a duty owed to the person by another person, and
-- (c) the person who is or was suffering ... or a person claiming through that person would, but for this Act, have
-- been entitled to bring an action for the recovery of damages in respect of that dust-related condition or death,
-- proceedings for damages ... may be brought before the Tribunal and may not be brought or entertained before any other
-- court or tribunal." (2) a duty includes a statutory duty; (5) a person claiming through includes a relative under the
-- Compensation to Relatives Act 1897; s 17(4) lets a legal personal representative bring proceedings as in their own
-- right. s 10(1): the jurisdiction is exclusive "except as provided by sections 29 and 32" (s 29 is repealed: finding
-- 2). "Would, but for this Act, have been entitled" is a question of the general law, asked as a fact. An "if": a
-- failed condition is JUST FALSE.
@export Must my claim for damages for a dust-related condition (or a death from one) be brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal, and in no other court (ss 10(1), 11(1), (2), (5), 17(4))?
GIVEN `my condition` IS A `The condition` @desc The condition the person suffers, suffered, or was suffering from immediately before death. One of the values of the dust-related-condition question
      `the person is suffering, has suffered, or was suffering immediately before death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is suffering or has suffered from the condition, or, if they have died, was suffering from it immediately before death
      `it is alleged the condition is attributable wholly or partly to a breach of duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim alleges the condition was caused wholly or partly by a breach of a duty (under the common law or a statute) owed to the person by someone else
      `who is claiming` IS A `Who is claiming` @desc Who is bringing the claim. One of: "the person with the condition", "their executor, administrator or other legal personal representative", "a relative for whose benefit a Compensation to Relatives Act 1897 action may be brought", "another person claiming through the person with the condition", "someone with no claim through the person with the condition"
      `but for this Act an action for damages could be brought` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, under the general law and apart from this Act, the claimant would be entitled to sue for damages for the condition or the death
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my claim be brought in the Dust Diseases Tribunal` MEANS
    IF     `is a dust-related condition` `my condition`
       AND `the person is suffering, has suffered, or was suffering immediately before death`
       AND `it is alleged the condition is attributable wholly or partly to a breach of duty`
       AND `the claimant is the person or claims through them`
       AND `but for this Act an action for damages could be brought`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE  -- an "if" whose conditions fail
    WHERE
        `the claimant is the person or claims through them` MEANS
            CONSIDER `who is claiming`
            WHEN `someone with no claim through the person with the condition` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 11(3) "If the cause of action giving rise to proceedings ... also gives rise to a claim in respect of some other
-- matter, the claim may be included in those proceedings even though it does not relate to a dust-related condition";
-- (4) "Any matter that is ancillary or related to a matter that is the subject of proceedings ... may also be included".
-- A grant limited by its own words: outside it, NOTHING.
@export Can I include this other claim in my Dust Diseases Tribunal proceedings (s 11(3), (4))?
GIVEN `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your main claim is a claim for damages for a dust-related condition or death that section 11(1) sends to the Tribunal
      `the other claim arises from the same cause of action` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other claim arises from the same cause of action as your dust-related claim, even if it is not about a dust-related condition
      `the other claim is ancillary or related to my claim` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the other claim is ancillary or related to the matter of your dust-related claim
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I include this other claim in my Tribunal proceedings` MEANS
    IF `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` AND (`the other claim arises from the same cause of action` OR `the other claim is ancillary or related to my claim`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own words; the Act is silent on other claims)

-- s 10(6) declares proceedings under s 11 (or transferred under s 12) "excluded matters" for s 5F of the Corporations Act
-- 2001 in relation to ss 471B and 500(2) of that Act (which would otherwise need the leave of the Court supervising the
-- winding up), and (7) declares them an "applied Corporations legislation matter" so that those sections apply as State
-- law "as if a reference in those sections to the Court were a reference to the Tribunal". Read together: the leave
-- needed is the Tribunal's, not the supervising Court's (fork F12).
@export The company I want to sue is being wound up or is under external administration: whose leave do I need to bring or continue my Tribunal claim (s 10(6), (7))?
GIVEN `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a dust-related claim that section 11(1) sends to the Tribunal (or that was transferred under section 12)
      `the Corporations Act would require leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the company is being wound up or under another external administration such that section 471B or 500(2) of the Corporations Act 2001 would otherwise require the leave of the Court to begin or continue proceedings against it
GIVETH A MAYBE `Whose leave I need against the company`
`Whose leave do I need to sue the company` MEANS
    IF `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` AND `the Corporations Act would require leave`
    THEN JUST `the Tribunal's leave, not the leave of the Court supervising the administration`
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 10(6), (7) reach only s 11 or s 12 proceedings that would need that leave)

§§ `s 11A -- provisional damages`

-- s 11A(1) "This section applies to proceedings of the kind referred to in section 11 (1) that are brought after the
-- commencement of this section and in which there is proved or admitted to be a chance that at some definite or
-- indefinite time in the future the [injured person] will, as a result or partly as a result of the breach of duty ...,
-- develop another dust-related condition." (2) "The Tribunal may, in accordance with the rules--(a) award damages
-- assessed on the assumption that the injured person will not develop another dust-related condition, and (b) award
-- further damages at a future date if the injured person does develop another dust-related condition." "Applies to
-- proceedings ... in which": a scope limit with no "only"; outside it, NOTHING (fork F3).
@export Can the Tribunal award me damages now on the footing that I will not develop another dust-related condition, with further damages later if I do (s 11A)?
GIVEN `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim for damages for a dust-related condition that section 11(1) sends to the Tribunal
      `my proceedings were brought after section 11A commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceedings were brought after section 11A of the Act commenced (inserted by amendment in the 1990s; true for proceedings brought in this century)
      `the chance of another condition is proved or admitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is proved or admitted that there is a chance that, at some time in the future, you will develop another dust-related condition as a result (or partly as a result) of the same breach of duty
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be awarded provisional damages` MEANS
    IF `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` AND `my proceedings were brought after section 11A commenced` AND `the chance of another condition is proved or admitted`
    THEN JUST TRUE  -- "may", in accordance with the rules
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a scope limit with no "only"; fork F3)

@export I was awarded provisional damages: can the Tribunal now award me further damages for another dust-related condition (s 11A(2)(b))?
GIVEN `I was awarded damages under section 11A(2)(a)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal earlier awarded you damages under section 11A(2)(a), on the assumption that you would not develop another dust-related condition
      `I have developed another dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have since developed another dust-related condition
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be awarded further damages for another condition` MEANS
    IF (NOT `I was awarded damages under section 11A(2)(a)`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 11A(2)(b) speaks only of an injured person awarded damages under (a))
    ELSE IF `I have developed another dust-related condition`
         THEN JUST TRUE  -- "may", in accordance with the rules (s 33(4)(b1))
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "if the injured person does develop": not yet

§§ `s 12 -- transfer from the Supreme Court or District Court`

-- s 12(1) "If--(a) on the commencement of this section, proceedings of the kind referred to in section 11 (1) are
-- pending in the Supreme Court and the hearing of those proceedings has not begun, or (b) after that commencement,
-- proceedings of that kind are brought or are pending in the Supreme Court, the registrar ... must transfer the
-- proceedings to the Tribunal, together with any ancillary or related matters." (2) is the same for the District Court.
-- Proceedings pending at commencement whose hearing had begun are outside (a) but, read literally, inside "are pending"
-- after commencement in (b): the two paragraphs disagree (finding 3), so that case is NOTHING (fork F4).
@export If my dust-related claim was started in the Supreme Court or the District Court, must it be transferred to the Dust Diseases Tribunal (s 12)?
GIVEN `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your claim is a claim for damages for a dust-related condition or death of the kind section 11(1) describes
      `the court my case is in` IS A `The court my case is in` @desc The court your proceedings were started in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "some other court or tribunal"
      `when my case began` IS A `When my case began` @desc When the proceedings were started, relative to the commencement of section 12 (in 1989). One of: "brought after section 12 commenced", "pending when section 12 commenced, its hearing not yet begun", "pending when section 12 commenced, its hearing already begun"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my case be transferred to the Tribunal` MEANS
    IF (NOT `my claim is one that must be brought in the Tribunal`)
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- "if ... proceedings of the kind referred to in section 11 (1)"
    ELSE CONSIDER `the court my case is in`
         WHEN `some other court or tribunal` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 12 names only the Supreme and District Courts; s 11(1) bars the other court from entertaining the claim)
         OTHERWISE
             CONSIDER `when my case began`
             WHEN `pending when section 12 commenced, its hearing already begun` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (paras (a) and (b) disagree; fork F4)
             OTHERWISE JUST TRUE  -- the registrar "must transfer", with any ancillary or related matters

§§ `s 12A -- no limitation period`

-- s 12A(1) "The purpose of this section is to enable proceedings to be brought before the Tribunal in relation to
-- dust-related conditions at any time." (2) "Nothing in the Limitation Act 1969 or any other statute of limitations
-- operates to prevent the bringing or maintenance of proceedings before the Tribunal in relation to dust-related
-- conditions." (3) even if a limitation period has already expired, and as if Div 1 of Part 4 of that Act had never been
-- in force. Sch 3 cl 7(2): s 12A does not apply "to the extent that proceedings on the cause of action have been
-- determined before the commencement of that section". A permission with no WITHIN: time alone never ends it.
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Tribunal party` `Tribunal act`
`s 12A -- proceedings may be brought before the Tribunal at any time` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MAY `bring proceedings before the Tribunal`
    HENCE FULFILLED

@export Can a limitation period stop me bringing my dust-related claim in the Tribunal on the day I plan to, however long ago my cause of action arose (s 12A, Sch 3 cl 7)?
GIVEN `my proceedings are in the Tribunal about a dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are (or will be) before the Dust Diseases Tribunal in relation to a dust-related condition
      `my cause of action was determined before section 12A commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether proceedings on this same cause of action were already decided before section 12A commenced (in 1998)
      `the day my cause of action arose` IS A DATE @desc The day your cause of action arose (for example the day of the exposure, the first symptoms or the diagnosis)
      `the day I plan to bring proceedings` IS A DATE @desc The day you plan to file (or have filed) proceedings in the Tribunal
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a limitation period stop my Tribunal claim` MEANS
    IF `my proceedings are in the Tribunal about a dust-related condition` AND (NOT `my cause of action was determined before section 12A commenced`)
    THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE `s 12A -- proceedings may be brought before the Tribunal at any time` 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `bring proceedings before the Tribunal` `the time of filing`))
         WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST FALSE  -- "nothing in the Limitation Act 1969 or any other statute of limitations operates to prevent"
         OTHERWISE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (not reached: a permission without a deadline is always fulfilled by the act)
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 12A does not reach the case; the Limitation Act 1969, a separate subject, decides)
    WHERE
        `the time of filing` MEANS `not below zero` (`the days from` `the day my cause of action arose` `to` `the day I plan to bring proceedings`)

§§ `s 12B -- non-economic damages after the plaintiff's death`

-- s 12B(1) "The purpose of this section is to enable the estate of a person whose death has been caused by a
-- dust-related condition to recover damages for the person's pain or suffering, or for any bodily or mental harm
-- suffered by the person, or for curtailment of the person's expectation of life, provided proceedings commenced by the
-- person were pending before the Tribunal at the person's death." (2) "Section 2 (2) (d) of the Law Reform (Miscellaneous
-- Provisions) Act 1944 does not apply in relation to proceedings commenced by a person before his or her death and
-- pending before the Tribunal at his or her death, where the cause of action is for damages in respect of a
-- dust-related condition." Where the proviso fails, the 1944 Act's bar applies: JUST FALSE (fork F5).
@export After the death of a person with a dust-related condition, can their estate recover these damages in the proceedings they started (s 12B)?
GIVEN `the cause of action is for damages for a dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person's claim was for damages in respect of a dust-related condition
      `the person started the proceedings before they died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person who died had commenced the proceedings themselves, before their death
      `the proceedings were pending in the Tribunal at the death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether those proceedings were still pending (not finally decided, settled or discontinued) before the Dust Diseases Tribunal when the person died
      `the head of damages` IS A `The head of damages after death` @desc The damages the estate wants. One of: "the person's pain or suffering", "bodily or mental harm the person suffered", "curtailment of the person's expectation of life", "some other head of damages"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the estate recover these damages` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the head of damages`
    WHEN `some other head of damages` THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 12B speaks only of the s 2(2)(d) heads; the 1944 Act decides the rest)
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the cause of action is for damages for a dust-related condition` AND `the person started the proceedings before they died` AND `the proceedings were pending in the Tribunal at the death`
        THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 2(2)(d) of the 1944 Act does not apply
        ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "provided ... pending"; s 2(2)(d) of the 1944 Act applies (fork F5)

§§ `s 12C(1) -- settling with some of those liable`

-- s 12C(1) "For the avoidance of doubt, settlement with one or more joint tortfeasors in or in relation to proceedings
-- before the Tribunal and who are liable in respect of damage as a result of a dust-related condition is not a bar to
-- recovery against one or more other joint tortfeasors (whether or not they are defendants in the proceedings), unless
-- the terms of the settlement otherwise provide." s 3: settlement "includes compromise or agreement". The "unless" once
-- met: the settlement's own terms bar recovery, JUST TRUE (fork F6).
@export Does my settlement with one or more of those jointly liable stop me recovering from the others (s 12C(1))?
GIVEN `the settlement is in or about Tribunal proceedings for a dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you settled (including by compromise or agreement) with one or more joint tortfeasors liable for damage from a dust-related condition, in or in relation to proceedings before the Dust Diseases Tribunal
      `the settlement terms say it bars recovery from the others` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the terms of the settlement themselves provide that it bars you from recovering against the other joint tortfeasors
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does my settlement bar recovery from the others` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the settlement is in or about Tribunal proceedings for a dust-related condition`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 12C speaks only of settlements in or about Tribunal proceedings)
    ELSE IF `the settlement terms say it bars recovery from the others`
         THEN JUST TRUE  -- "unless the terms of the settlement otherwise provide" (fork F6)
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "is not a bar"

§§ `s 12D -- no deduction of dust diseases compensation`

-- s 12D(3) "damages for non-economic loss means damages or compensation for the following--(a) pain and suffering,
-- (b) loss of amenities of life, (c) loss of expectation of life, (d) disfigurement, (e) the need for services of a
-- domestic nature or services relating to nursing and attendance which have been or are to be provided to a person by
-- another person, and for which the first person has not paid and is not liable to pay any fee or charge." A "means"
-- definition: outside it, JUST FALSE.
@export Is this part of my damages "damages for non-economic loss" (s 12D(3))?
GIVEN `the part of my damages` IS A `The part of my damages` @desc The part of your damages. One of: "pain and suffering", "loss of amenities of life", "loss of expectation of life", "disfigurement", "domestic, nursing or attendance services provided to me by another person", "economic loss or expenses"
      `I paid or must pay for the services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For domestic, nursing or attendance services: whether you have paid, or are liable to pay, a fee or charge for them
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this damages for non-economic loss` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the part of my damages`
    WHEN `economic loss or expenses` THEN JUST FALSE  -- a "means" definition
    WHEN `domestic, nursing or attendance services provided to me by another person` THEN
        IF `I paid or must pay for the services` THEN JUST FALSE ELSE JUST TRUE  -- "for which the first person has not paid and is not liable to pay"
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 12D(1) "This section applies to proceedings before the Tribunal (including proceedings on an appeal from the
-- Tribunal) for damages in relation to dust-related conditions." (2) "In determining damages for non-economic loss in
-- any such proceedings, no deduction is to be made for any amount of compensation already paid or payable, or payable
-- in the future, under the Workers' Compensation (Dust Diseases) Act 1942." A flat "no deduction": JUST FALSE.
@export Can my damages for non-economic loss be reduced because of this compensation (s 12D)?
GIVEN `my proceedings are in the Tribunal for a dust-related condition` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your proceedings are before the Dust Diseases Tribunal (or on an appeal from it) for damages in relation to a dust-related condition
      `the part of my damages` IS A `The part of my damages` @desc The part of your damages. One of: "pain and suffering", "loss of amenities of life", "loss of expectation of life", "disfigurement", "domestic, nursing or attendance services provided to me by another person", "economic loss or expenses"
      `I paid or must pay for the services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For domestic, nursing or attendance services: whether you have paid, or are liable to pay, a fee or charge for them
      `the compensation` IS A `The compensation paid or payable` @desc The compensation already paid, payable now, or payable in the future. One of: "compensation under the Workers' Compensation (Dust Diseases) Act 1942", "some other compensation or benefit"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my non-economic damages be reduced for this compensation` MEANS
    IF     `my proceedings are in the Tribunal for a dust-related condition`
       AND `Is this damages for non-economic loss` `the part of my damages` `I paid or must pay for the services` EQUALS JUST TRUE
       AND `the compensation is under the 1942 Act`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- "no deduction is to be made"
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 12D is silent; the general law of damages decides)
    WHERE
        `the compensation is under the 1942 Act` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the compensation`
            WHEN `compensation under the Workers' Compensation (Dust Diseases) Act 1942` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

§§ `s 14 -- enforcing a money judgment`

-- s 14(1) "If a judgment or order of the Tribunal is for payment of an amount of money (including a sum awarded as costs)
-- the judgment or order shall, on the filing of the prescribed documents in the registry of the Common Law Division of
-- the Supreme Court, be taken to be a judgment of that Court". (2) the prescribed documents: (a) a copy certified by the
-- registrar, (b) an affidavit by the person to be paid "specifying the amount unpaid ... and, where the judgment or order
-- is to take effect on a default, as to the making of the default". (3) "No fee is payable for the filing". s 10(5): a
-- decision of the Tribunal is otherwise enforced as a Supreme Court decision, subject to s 14.
@export Is my Tribunal money judgment now taken to be a Supreme Court judgment, and what must I file (s 14)?
GIVEN `the judgment is for payment of money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's judgment or order is for payment of an amount of money to you (including costs)
      `I filed a copy certified by the registrar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have filed in the Common Law Division registry of the Supreme Court a copy of the judgment certified by the Tribunal's registrar as a true copy
      `I filed an affidavit of the amount unpaid` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have filed there your affidavit specifying the amount unpaid under the judgment
      `the judgment takes effect on a default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the judgment or order is to take effect only on a default (for example, if instalments are not paid)
      `my affidavit deposes to the default` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it takes effect on a default: whether your affidavit also deposes to the making of the default
GIVETH A MAYBE `What my money judgment becomes`
`What does my money judgment become` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the judgment is for payment of money`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 14 is about money judgments; s 10(5) governs the rest)
    ELSE IF `I filed a copy certified by the registrar` AND `I filed an affidavit of the amount unpaid` AND `the default is dealt with`
         THEN JUST `taken to be a Supreme Court judgment for the money, and no filing fee is payable`
         ELSE JUST `not yet: file the certified copy and the affidavit in the Common Law Division registry of the Supreme Court`
    WHERE
        `the default is dealt with` MEANS (NOT `the judgment takes effect on a default`) OR `my affidavit deposes to the default`

§§ `s 17 -- parties`

-- s 17(1) "If a party to proceedings before the Tribunal has a right to proceed against two or more persons who may be
-- jointly liable--(a) it is sufficient if at least one of those persons is served with process in the proceedings, and
-- (b) a decision in the proceedings may be given and enforced against the person or persons found to be liable."
-- (2) "Subsection (1) is subject to the rules." The rules are not deposited.
@export Can my case go ahead if I have served only some of the people who may be jointly liable (s 17(1))?
GIVEN `how many may be jointly liable` IS A NUMBER @desc How many persons you have a right to proceed against who may be jointly liable
      `how many I have served` IS A NUMBER @desc How many of them you have served with process in the proceedings
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is service on some of those jointly liable enough` MEANS
    IF `how many may be jointly liable` AT LEAST 2 AND `how many I have served` AT LEAST 1
    THEN JUST TRUE  -- "it is sufficient if at least one ... is served", subject to the rules
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 17(1) is silent where none is served, or where only one person may be liable)

§§ `s 19 -- appearance and representation`

-- s 19(1) "A party ...--(a) may appear in the proceedings personally or by an Australian legal practitioner, and (b) may
-- not, except by leave of the Tribunal, be represented ... otherwise than by an Australian legal practitioner."
@export Can I appear myself, or have this person represent me, in the Dust Diseases Tribunal (s 19(1))?
GIVEN `who appears for me` IS A `Who appears for me` @desc Who would appear for you. One of: "I appear myself", "an Australian legal practitioner appears for me", "someone who is not an Australian legal practitioner appears for me"
      `the Tribunal gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For someone who is not an Australian legal practitioner: whether the Tribunal has given leave for them to represent you
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can this person appear for me` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who appears for me`
    WHEN `someone who is not an Australian legal practitioner appears for me` THEN
        IF `the Tribunal gave leave` THEN JUST TRUE ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "may not, except by leave"
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

-- s 19(2) "Only an Australian legal practitioner is entitled to demand or receive a fee or reward for representing a
-- party in proceedings before the Tribunal." A list closed by "only" (fork F7 for the practitioner).
@export Can the person representing me in the Tribunal charge me a fee (s 19(2))?
GIVEN `who represents me` IS A `Who is paid for representing me` @desc Who represents you. One of: "an Australian legal practitioner representing me", "someone else representing me"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my representative charge a fee` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who represents me`
    WHEN `an Australian legal practitioner representing me` THEN JUST TRUE  -- fork F7
    OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- "only an Australian legal practitioner is entitled"

§§ `s 20 -- my subpoenas and conduct money`

-- s 20(1): "If ... a party ... so requests, the registrar must issue a subpoena". (6) "If a subpoena is issued under
-- subsection (1) at the request of a party, the person specified in the subpoena is not required to comply with the
-- subpoena unless, not later than a reasonable time before the day on which the subpoena is to be complied with, that
-- person is tendered an amount, prescribed by, or calculated in accordance with, the rules, to meet the expense of
-- complying". The amount (the rules are not deposited) and "a reasonable time" are inputs. Time 0 is the day the
-- subpoena issues, in days; the tender is a condition on compliance, written with DO (fork F8).
GIVEN `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER, `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Tribunal party` `Tribunal act`
`s 20(6) -- conduct money is to be tendered a reasonable time before the day` `the prescribed amount` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    DO `tender conduct money to the person subpoenaed` `the sum` PROVIDED `the sum` AT LEAST `the prescribed amount`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "conduct money of the prescribed amount was not tendered a reasonable time before the day for compliance; the person subpoenaed need not comply (s 20(6))"

@export Must the person I had subpoenaed comply with the subpoena, given the conduct money I tendered (s 20(1), (6))?
GIVEN `the subpoena was issued at my request` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the subpoena was issued at your request (false if the Tribunal directed it)
      `the day the subpoena was issued` IS A DATE @desc The day the subpoena was issued
      `the day for compliance` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the subpoena is to be complied with
      `the reasonable time in days` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days before the day for compliance count as "a reasonable time" (a judgment for the Tribunal; give its view or your best estimate)
      `the prescribed amount` IS A NUMBER @desc The conduct money prescribed by, or calculated under, the rules, in dollars
      `I tendered conduct money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you tendered conduct money to the person subpoenaed
      `the amount I tendered` IS A NUMBER @desc How much you tendered, in dollars
      `the day I tendered it` IS A DATE @desc The day you tendered it
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must the person I subpoenaed comply` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the subpoena was issued at my request`)
    THEN JUST TRUE  -- s 20(6) does not reach a subpoena the Tribunal directed; the subpoena "requires" (s 20(1))
    ELSE IF (NOT `I tendered conduct money`)
         THEN JUST FALSE  -- "not required to comply ... unless"
         ELSE CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 20(6) -- conduct money is to be tendered a reasonable time before the day` `the prescribed amount` `the days allowed`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` (`tender conduct money to the person subpoenaed` `the amount I tendered`) `the tender time`))
              WHEN FULFILLED THEN JUST TRUE
              OTHERWISE JUST FALSE  -- "not required to comply ... unless"
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `not below zero` ((`the days from` `the day the subpoena was issued` `to` `the day for compliance`) MINUS `the reasonable time in days`)
        `the tender time` MEANS `not below zero` (`the days from` `the day the subpoena was issued` `to` `the day I tendered it`)

§§ `s 22 -- copies of my documents the Tribunal keeps`

-- s 22(2) "If a document or statement is produced before, and retained by, the Tribunal, the person otherwise entitled
-- to possession ... is, on request, entitled to be supplied, as soon as practicable, with a copy ... certified by the
-- registrar". (4): that person may also inspect, copy and take extracts at the times and places the registrar directs.
@export The Tribunal has kept a document of mine: am I entitled to a certified copy (s 22(2), (4))?
GIVEN `the Tribunal retained the document` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the document or statement was produced before, and retained by, the Tribunal
      `I am otherwise entitled to possession of it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are the person who would otherwise be entitled to possession of it
      `I have asked for a copy` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have asked for a copy
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to a certified copy` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the Tribunal retained the document`) OR (NOT `I am otherwise entitled to possession of it`)
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 22(2) is silent on other documents and other persons)
    ELSE IF `I have asked for a copy`
         THEN JUST TRUE  -- as soon as practicable, and to inspect and copy it (s 22(4))
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "on request": not until asked

§§ `s 24 -- evidence taken early`

-- s 24(1) "The Tribunal may, on the application of a party ..., authorise [a judge, counsel, commissioner for affidavits,
-- or consular officer] to take ... the examination of a witness de bene esse, if the witness--(c) is absent from New
-- South Wales, or (d) is expected to die or to be unable from sickness or infirmity to attend at the hearing". The
-- witness may be the claimant. An "if": a failed condition is JUST FALSE; the authorisation is the Tribunal's ("may").
@export Can I apply for a witness's evidence (including my own) to be taken before the hearing, because they are away or may die or be too ill to attend (s 24)?
GIVEN `the witness is absent from New South Wales` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the witness (who may be you) is absent from New South Wales
      `the witness is expected to die or be unable to attend` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the witness is expected to die, or to be unable from sickness or infirmity to attend the hearing
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the evidence be taken early` MEANS
    IF `the witness is absent from New South Wales` OR `the witness is expected to die or be unable to attend`
    THEN JUST TRUE  -- the Tribunal "may" authorise it on your application
    ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "if the witness ..."

§§ `ss 25, 25A, 25B -- evidence, material and issues from other proceedings`

-- s 25(3) "Historical evidence and general medical evidence concerning dust exposure and dust diseases which has been
-- admitted in any proceedings before the Tribunal may, with the leave of the Tribunal, be received as evidence in any
-- other proceedings before the Tribunal, whether or not the proceedings are between the same parties." A grant limited
-- by its own words: outside it, NOTHING (s 25(1), (2) then apply the Supreme Court's rules of evidence).
@export Can evidence admitted in another Dust Diseases Tribunal case be used in mine (s 25(3))?
GIVEN `the evidence` IS A `The evidence from another case` @desc The kind of evidence. One of: "historical evidence concerning dust exposure and dust diseases", "general medical evidence concerning dust exposure and dust diseases", "evidence particular to the other case"
      `it was admitted in other Tribunal proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the evidence was admitted in other proceedings before the Dust Diseases Tribunal (between any parties)
      `the Tribunal gives leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal gives leave for it to be received in your case
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can evidence from another case be used in mine` MEANS
    IF `the evidence is general` AND `it was admitted in other Tribunal proceedings` AND `the Tribunal gives leave`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own words; s 25(1), (2) decide)
    WHERE
        `the evidence is general` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the evidence`
            WHEN `evidence particular to the other case` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 25A(1) "Material obtained for the purposes of proceedings before the Tribunal by discovery or interrogatories may--
-- (a) with the leave of the Tribunal, and (b) with the consent of--(i) ... the party who originally obtained the material
-- or the party's Australian legal practitioner, or (ii) another person prescribed by the rules, be used in other
-- proceedings before the Tribunal". (2) The rules may exclude kinds of proceedings. Sch 3 cl 11 (material obtained before
-- s 25A commenced, in 1998) is out (spent).
@export Can material another party obtained by discovery or interrogatories in another Tribunal case be used in mine (s 25A)?
GIVEN `it was obtained by discovery or interrogatories for Tribunal proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the material was obtained by discovery or interrogatories for the purposes of proceedings before the Dust Diseases Tribunal
      `the Tribunal gives leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal gives leave
      `the right person consents` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party who originally obtained the material (or their lawyer), or another person the rules prescribe, consents
      `the rules exclude my proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's rules exclude your kind of proceedings or circumstances from section 25A
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can material from another case be used in mine` MEANS
    IF `it was obtained by discovery or interrogatories for Tribunal proceedings` AND `the Tribunal gives leave` AND `the right person consents` AND (NOT `the rules exclude my proceedings`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (a grant limited by its own words)

-- s 25B(1) "Issues of a general nature determined in proceedings before the Tribunal (including proceedings on an appeal
-- from the Tribunal) may not be relitigated or reargued in other proceedings before the Tribunal without the leave of the
-- Tribunal, whether or not the proceedings are between the same parties." (2) leave considers new evidence, the conduct of
-- the earlier proceedings and other matters: the leave is an input. (3) the rules may exclude kinds of proceedings or
-- issues. A flat "may not ... without leave": JUST FALSE without leave.
@export Can a general issue the Tribunal has already decided in another case be argued again in mine (s 25B)?
GIVEN `the issue is general and was decided in Tribunal proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the issue is one of a general nature already determined in proceedings before the Dust Diseases Tribunal (or on appeal from it), between any parties
      `the rules exclude it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal's rules exclude the issue or your proceedings from section 25B
      `the Tribunal gives leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal gives leave to relitigate or reargue it (it considers new evidence, how the earlier proceedings were conducted, and other relevant matters)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can a decided general issue be reargued in my case` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the issue is general and was decided in Tribunal proceedings`) OR `the rules exclude it`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 25B is silent on other issues)
    ELSE IF `the Tribunal gives leave`
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE  -- "may not be relitigated or reargued ... without the leave of the Tribunal"

§§ `ss 13(5), 32 -- appeal to the Supreme Court`

-- s 32(1) "A party who is dissatisfied with a decision of the Tribunal in point of law or on a question as to the
-- admission or rejection of evidence may appeal to the Supreme Court." (4) "The following appeals ... may be made only
-- by leave of the Supreme Court--(a) an appeal from an interlocutory decision, (b) ... a decision as to costs only,
-- (c) ... a final decision, other than an appeal that involves (directly or indirectly) a claim for, or a question
-- relating to, an amount of $20,000 or more, (d) ... a decision made with the consent of the parties." s 13(5): a decision
-- is not liable "to be questioned or appealed against in any court, except as provided by section 32 of this Act or
-- section 48 of the Supreme Court Act 1970". The time to appeal is in rules not deposited.
@export Can I appeal against the Tribunal's decision to the Supreme Court, and do I need leave (ss 13(5), 32)?
GIVEN `the ground of my appeal` IS A `The ground of my appeal` @desc What you are dissatisfied with. One of: "a point of law", "the admission or rejection of evidence", "a question of fact only"
      `the decision` IS A `The decision I would appeal` @desc The kind of decision. One of: "an interlocutory decision", "a decision as to costs only", "a final decision", "a decision made with the consent of the parties"
      `the amount involved` IS A NUMBER @desc For a final decision: the largest amount, in dollars, that the appeal involves directly or indirectly (a claim for it, or a question relating to it)
GIVETH A `My appeal`
`Can I appeal and do I need leave` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the ground of my appeal`
    WHEN `a question of fact only` THEN `no appeal: section 13(5) leaves only section 48 of the Supreme Court Act 1970`
    OTHERWISE
        CONSIDER `the decision`
        WHEN `a final decision` THEN
            IF `the amount involved` AT LEAST 20000
            THEN `I may appeal to the Supreme Court as of right`
            ELSE `I may appeal to the Supreme Court only with its leave`
        OTHERWISE `I may appeal to the Supreme Court only with its leave`

§§ `s 32I -- information about my claim`

-- s 32I(1) "An Australian legal practitioner who acts for a party on a claim must provide the Registrar with such
-- information concerning the claim as the regulations may require". (2) "If both a barrister and a solicitor act for a
-- party, the obligation ... falls on the solicitor and not the barrister." (4) "If a party to a claim is not represented
-- on the claim by an Australian legal practitioner, the party must provide the information required by this section (as
-- and when an Australian legal practitioner representing the party would be required to provide it). Maximum penalty--
-- 5 penalty units." The regulations that fix what and when are not deposited: the days allowed are an input.
@export Who must give the Registrar the information about my claim that the regulations require (s 32I(1), (2), (4))?
GIVEN `a legal practitioner represents me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Australian legal practitioner (a solicitor or barrister) represents you on the claim
      `both a barrister and a solicitor act for me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether both a barrister and a solicitor act for you
GIVETH A `Who must give the claim information`
`Who must give the Registrar my claim information` MEANS
    IF `a legal practitioner represents me`
    THEN IF `both a barrister and a solicitor act for me`
         THEN `my solicitor, not my barrister`
         ELSE `my Australian legal practitioner`
    ELSE `I must, because no Australian legal practitioner represents me`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Tribunal party` `Tribunal act`
`s 32I(4) -- an unrepresented party must give the claim information` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the claimant`
    MUST `give the claim information to the Registrar`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the claimant` BECAUSE "the information the regulations require was not given to the Registrar when a legal practitioner would have had to give it (s 32I(4)); maximum penalty 5 penalty units"

@export I have no lawyer: have I given the Registrar the claim information in time (s 32I(4))?
GIVEN `a legal practitioner represents me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an Australian legal practitioner represents you on the claim
      `the day the time began` IS A DATE @desc The day from which the regulations count the time to give the information (for example the day the claim was filed)
      `the days the regulations allow` IS A NUMBER @desc How many days the regulations allow a legal practitioner to give the information (the regulations are not part of this encoding)
      `I have given the information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have given the Registrar the information
      `the day I gave it` IS A DATE @desc The day you gave it (any day if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
GIVETH A `What section 32I(4) means for me`
`Have I given the claim information in time` MEANS
    IF `a legal practitioner represents me`
    THEN `section 32I(4) puts no duty on me, because a legal practitioner represents me`
    ELSE IF `I have given the information`
         THEN CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`s 32I(4) -- an unrepresented party must give the claim information` `the days the regulations allow`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the claimant` `give the claim information to the Registrar` `the time given`))
              WHEN FULFILLED THEN `I gave the information within the time`
              OTHERWISE `I have not given the information within the time: an offence, maximum penalty 5 penalty units`
         ELSE IF `the days from` `the day the time began` `to` `today` AT MOST `the days the regulations allow`
              THEN `I have not yet given the information, and the time has not ended`
              ELSE `I have not given the information within the time: an offence, maximum penalty 5 penalty units`
    WHERE
        `the time given` MEANS `not below zero` (`the days from` `the day the time began` `to` `the day I gave it`)

-- s 32I(8) "A person who in the course of the administration of this Act obtains information provided to the Registrar
-- under subsection (1) (not including information included in a report under subsection (6)) must not directly or
-- indirectly make a record of the information or divulge it to another person except--(a) with the consent of the person
-- who provided the information ..., or (b) in connection with the administration or execution of this Act, or (c) in
-- accordance with a requirement imposed by or under a law of the State (other than a requirement imposed by a subpoena or
-- other compulsory process) or of the Commonwealth. Maximum penalty--50 penalty units." A flat "must not ... except".
@export Can someone administering the Act record or pass on the claim information given to the Registrar, for this reason (s 32I(8))?
GIVEN `it is in a report to the Attorney General` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the information is included in a report the Registrar gave the Attorney General under section 32I(6)
      `the reason` IS A `Why the information would be recorded or divulged` @desc Why it would be recorded or passed on. One of: "with the consent of the person who provided it", "in connection with the administration or execution of the Act", "a requirement of a NSW or Commonwealth law, other than a subpoena or other compulsory process", "a subpoena or other compulsory process", "some other reason"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my claim information be recorded or divulged` MEANS
    IF `it is in a report to the Attorney General`
    THEN NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 32I(8) excludes information in a report under (6))
    ELSE CONSIDER `the reason`
         WHEN `a subpoena or other compulsory process` THEN JUST FALSE  -- "must not ... (other than a requirement imposed by a subpoena or other compulsory process)"; maximum penalty 50 penalty units
         WHEN `some other reason` THEN JUST FALSE  -- "must not ... except"; maximum penalty 50 penalty units
         OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `Part 6 -- interim payments`

-- s 41(1) Division 5 of Part 6 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 (interim payments) "extends to proceedings before the
-- Tribunal in relation to any liability of an employer to or in respect of whom section 151AB of the Workers Compensation
-- Act 1987 applies." (2) "However, that Division does not apply to any claim in respect of which an inter-insurer dispute
-- remains unresolved." s 43(1) "While an inter-insurer dispute remains unresolved, the Tribunal may ... order a designated
-- insurer who is a party to the dispute to make one or more payments to the plaintiff". (2) on the plaintiff's
-- application at any stage. (3) "if--(a) the defendant or the designated insurer has admitted liability, or (b) the
-- plaintiff has obtained judgment against the defendant ..., or (c) the Tribunal is satisfied that, if the proceedings
-- proceeded to trial, the plaintiff would obtain judgment for substantial damages against the defendant." s 40: an
-- inter-insurer dispute is one under s 151AC of the 1987 Act; asked as a fact.
@export Can I get interim payments of my damages before my case is finished, and under which provision (ss 41, 43)?
GIVEN `an inter-insurer dispute remains unresolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether there is an unresolved dispute between insurers under section 151AC of the Workers Compensation Act 1987 about which of them is liable for your claim
      `section 151AB applies to the employer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the claim is about the liability of an employer to whom section 151AB of the Workers Compensation Act 1987 applies
      `liability has been admitted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant or the designated insurer has admitted liability
      `I have judgment against the defendant` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have obtained judgment against the defendant (whether or not damages have been assessed)
      `the Tribunal is satisfied I would get substantial damages` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Tribunal is satisfied that, if the case went to trial, you would obtain judgment for substantial damages against the defendant
GIVETH A MAYBE `Interim payments for me`
`Can I get interim payments` MEANS
    IF `an inter-insurer dispute remains unresolved`
    THEN IF `liability has been admitted` OR `I have judgment against the defendant` OR `the Tribunal is satisfied I would get substantial damages`
         THEN JUST `the Tribunal may order a designated insurer to make interim payments to me under section 43`
         ELSE JUST `section 43 applies, but none of its conditions for an order is met`
    ELSE IF `section 151AB applies to the employer`
         THEN JUST `Division 5 of Part 6 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 extends to my claim under section 41`
         ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (Part 6 is silent; the Civil Procedure Act 2005, a separate subject, decides)

-- s 43(4) "The Tribunal may order a designated insurer to make one or more payments of such amounts as it thinks just but
-- not exceeding--(a) if damages have not been assessed--a reasonable proportion of the damages that in the opinion of
-- the Tribunal are likely to be recovered by the plaintiff, or (b) if damages have been assessed--the amount of damages
-- as assessed." (5) the estimate takes account of contributory negligence and cross-claims. The ceiling is read as the
-- total of the payments (fork F9). The proportion and the estimate are the Tribunal's: inputs.
@export What is the most the interim payments ordered under section 43 can add up to (s 43(4), (5))?
GIVEN `my damages have been assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your damages have been assessed
      `the assessed damages` IS A NUMBER @desc If assessed: the amount of damages as assessed, in dollars
      `the likely damages` IS A NUMBER @desc If not assessed: the damages the Tribunal thinks you are likely to recover, in dollars, after allowing for any contributory negligence and cross-claims
      `the reasonable proportion` IS A NUMBER @desc If not assessed: the proportion of those likely damages the Tribunal considers reasonable, as a fraction (for example 0.5 for half)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the most the interim payments can be` MEANS
    IF `my damages have been assessed`
    THEN `the assessed damages`
    ELSE `the likely damages` TIMES `the reasonable proportion`

-- s 44(1) "The fact that a designated insurer makes one or more interim payments is not of itself an admission of
-- liability by the defendant or the designated insurer." (2) an order, or a refusal, "is not a finding as to liability".
@export Does an insurer's interim payment to me mean it has admitted liability (s 44)?
GIVEN `a designated insurer made the interim payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment was an interim payment by a designated insurer under Division 3 of Part 6 (ordered under section 43 or made voluntarily)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the interim payment an admission of liability` MEANS
    IF `a designated insurer made the interim payment`
    THEN JUST FALSE  -- "is not of itself an admission of liability"
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 44 speaks only of a designated insurer's interim payments)

-- s 45(1) "This section applies to proceedings in which a designated insurer makes one or more interim payments before
-- damages are assessed." (2) the Tribunal or an arbitrator or referee may order "(a) the repayment by the plaintiff of all
-- or part of any interim payment, with or without interest". (3) on final judgment, on leave to discontinue or withdraw,
-- or on any party's application. s 46 (payments after assessment) provides only for reimbursement between insurers.
@export Could I be ordered to repay an interim payment (ss 45, 46)?
GIVEN `a designated insurer made the interim payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment was an interim payment by a designated insurer under Division 3 of Part 6
      `it was made before my damages were assessed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment was made before your damages were assessed
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Could I be ordered to repay an interim payment` MEANS
    IF `a designated insurer made the interim payment` AND `it was made before my damages were assessed`
    THEN JUST TRUE  -- the Tribunal, arbitrator or referee "may" order repayment, with or without interest
    ELSE NOTHING  -- NOTHING-kind (s 46 is silent on repayment by the plaintiff; s 45 does not reach other payments)
